{
  "$comment": "#EDE-55 LOCAL PARITY (Local desktops, #EDE-54): the rules a LOCAL DESKTOP replays without a server. A Local desktop is one team in its own SQLite store (the IDE's synced-shape layout, every column TEXT, the same 34 tables), authoritative and offline; elocal's executor answers the tRPC procedures the clients call (`procedures.local`, the exact wire names) over that store, and refuses every other one with `procedures.refusal` (HTTP 501 on the Transport, so a client shows it as a plain refusal, never a sign-in). IDENTITY: the person at the machine is the one member and owner; their user id is minted once per desktop, their email is `<login>@<identity.emailDomain>` (`identity.cases`: lowercase, runs of anything but a-z 0-9 . _ - become one dash, trimmed, empty = local), which is what mentions and the team import rewrite. SEED (`seed.cases`, ids from the caller's id source in order): a new desktop holds its team row (id = the desktop id, slug = `seed.slugPrefix` + the first 8 characters of the desktop id), the identity's user row and owner membership, the contract's builtin statuses (`issueStatusDefaults`, in order) and one board (`seed.board`). ISSUES: numbers count per board from 1 (`issues.numberCases`: the next = max + 1), the identifier is `<prefix>-<number>` (`issues.identifierCases`), the status anchor follows the row's category (`issues.anchorByCategory`, the server's CATEGORY_ANCHOR) and `issues.create.cases` give the whole created row (status from `statusId`, else from the anchor `status`, else the builtin backlog; the assignee = the input's, else the identity (the sole member rule); creator = the identity; priority none; sort_order = the number; source user; created and updated = now); `issues.update.cases` apply a patch (a key present = set, null = cleared, a status by id dual-writes the anchor). A created issue keeps the input's `draftId` as `draft_id` (the draft it was filed from; that draft row goes). DRAFTS (#EDE-65, `drafts`): `issueDrafts.upsert` writes the person's draft row (`drafts.upsert.cases`: keyed by the client's id, user = the identity, title and description default empty, priority none, labels the full set, an agent assignee clears the person, a re-save keeps created_at), refused with `drafts.consumedRefusal` when an issue already carries that `draft_id`; `issueDrafts.delete` removes it (`deleted`: was there one); `issueDrafts.listAttachments` = none (a Local desktop's files ride an issue, never a draft). COMPOSER DRAFTS (#EXP-1800, composer-draft.json, the server's `composerDrafts` router): `composerDrafts.upsert {teamId, key, text}` writes the identity's one row per `key` (`composerDrafts.upsert.cases`: a new key mints the row's id, user = the identity, team = the input's, the text verbatim, created and updated = now; a re-save keeps the id and created_at and replaces team and text), refused with `composerDrafts.emptyRefusal` when the text is empty or only whitespace (that is a delete) and when the key does not parse (composer-draft.json `unparsable`); `composerDrafts.delete {key}` removes the identity's row for the key (`deleted`: was there one; deleting nothing is fine). The rows sit in the store's `composer_drafts` table, the shape's columns, so the composer reads them as it reads the server's. COMMENTS (`comments.cases`): author = the identity, audience team, source user, one level deep: a reply to a reply lands under the top comment. SESSIONS (`sessions.send.cases`): a send without a session makes a ROOT session (status queued until a local agent run takes it, #EDE-58; device = the desktop's device id; title = the name, else the text's first line cut at 80), its conversation (kind session) and the person's message (author user); a send into a session adds only the message; a send's `attachmentIds` ride the message, and the body gets the server's line for every one it does not name yet (`attachmentLine`: `![name](/api/attachments/{id})` for the five inline image types, else `[name](/api/attachments/{id})`, the name without brackets; the lines after the trimmed text, joined by blank lines; `attachments` = those rows as the desktop stores them, the bytes in its blob store), so the desktop's agent run reads them as the server's devices do (#EDE-65 lane L); `issueIds` link through session_issues (source user). AGENTS (`agents.assistant`): `ensureAssistant` makes the identity's one assistant row (name Assistant, role assistant, device = the desktop's) and answers it again after. Everything else a procedure writes keeps the server's column names; a local id is a uuid the client mints. ISSUE TRASH (#EXP-1799, issue-lifecycle.json `trash`, the server's semantics): `issues.delete` / `bulkDelete` move a live issue into the desktop's trash, stamping `deleted_at` (now), `deleted_by` (the identity) and `deleted_by_agent_id` (the calling session's agent, else null); nothing cascades: the issue row and its children (comments, attachments and their bytes, issue_labels, issue_subscribers, issue_events, notifications, session_issues, pull_request_issues, issue_relations on EITHER side) leave the synced tables for the sidecar's trash, so every local read (the shapes, issues.get, issues.search, the agent tools) hides them. `issues.listDeleted` = the trash, newest deleted first, `purgeAt` = deleted_at + retentionDays, an issue of a removed board not listed; `issues.restore` puts every row back (minus a label, session or related issue gone since; a relation whose other side is still trashed rides that issue) and clears the three columns; `issues.purge` (the identity is the owner) drops it now, the sweep (on open and on every trash read) once `isPurgeDue`; removing a board purges its trashed issues with it. Each writes the team stream's `issue.deleted` / `issue.restored` / `issue.purged` row (`events`, payload = the trash `eventPayload`; emitted by the session for a call that names one, `viaMcp` then, else the identity, the sweep = server with null actors). REPOSITORIES (#EXP-1727): a Local desktop links git working copies on the machine (`repositories.linkFolder {path, boardId?}`: the folder's name, the branch its HEAD names, the board pointed at it; `repositories.listLocal`; `repositories.forIssue` = the issue's board's linked folder or null). They live in the desktop's sidecar, never in a shape, and nothing clones or fetches: the folder is the clone.",
